Consider the differential equation \( y'' + \omega_0^2 y = M \cos \omega x + N \sin \omega x \), where \( M \), \( N \), \( \omega \), and \( \omega_0 \) are constants with \( \omega_0 \neq \omega \). Which of the following expressions gives the general solution for \( y(x) \)?

A) y = C_1 e^{\omega_0 x} + C_2 e^{-\omega_0 x} + \frac{M \cos(\omega x) + N \sin(\omega x)}{\omega_0^2 + \omega^2}
B) y = C_1 \cos(\omega x) + C_2 \sin(\omega x) + \frac{M \cos(\omega_0 x) + N \sin(\omega_0 x)}{\omega^2 - \omega_0^2}
C) y = C_1 \cos(\omega_0 x) + C_2 \sin(\omega_0 x) + \frac{M \cos(\omega x) + N \sin(\omega x)}{\omega_0^2 - \omega^2}
D) y = C_1 e^{i \omega_0 x} + C_2 e^{-i \omega_0 x} + M x \sin(\omega x) + N x \cos(\omega x)

A two-port device N has Z-parameters given by Z_{22} = N^2 Z_{11} and Z_{12} = Z_{21} = N Z_{11}. If a load Z_L is connected to the output terminal, what is the input impedance Z_{in} = V_1/I_1 as seen from the input port? Assume Z_{11} \gg Z_L/N^2. Which of the following correctly describes Z_{in} in this limit?

A) \frac{Z_L}{N^2}
B) \frac{Z_L}{N}
C) Z_{11} + \frac{Z_L}{N}
